{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-65990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n- $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978414/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b000e96c2ec257e4003ff0f930edd4c8418aacb8d35bc453f4cd017455e3b3"} {"question" "q-65990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978414/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b000e96c2ec257e4003ff0f930edd4c8418aacb8d35bc453f4cd017455e3b3"} {"question" "q-65990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978414/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d1e9e949959a5cdbc9bddaf72036a25a27cc953bf323c08ed9d4bb1dd8dc9047"} {"question" "q-65990" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978414/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b000e96c2ec257e4003ff0f930edd4c8418aacb8d35bc453f4cd017455e3b3"} {"question" "q-65990" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978414/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b000e96c2ec257e4003ff0f930edd4c8418aacb8d35bc453f4cd017455e3b3"} {"question" "q-65990" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978414/state.json" "file_sha256" "76b000e96c2ec257e4003ff0f930edd4c8418aacb8d35bc453f4cd017455e3b3"} {"question" "q-66008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Acade